<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Artaxata</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Artashat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Artaxata was the capital of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxias-i">Artaxias I</a> in the early 2nd century BCE on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a> river, near the modern town of Artashat. Ancient writers say <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> advised on its site. It remained the main royal city for centuries, was taken and burned by the Roman general Corbulo in 58 CE and later rebuilt, before giving way as capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vagharshapat">Vagharshapat</a>. Its remains lie near the monastery of Khor Virap.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/artaxata.jpg" alt="Artaxata" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vyacheslav Argenberg · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Ararat_and_Khor_Virap_Monastery_2,_Ararat_Plain,_Armenia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxias-i">Artaxias I</a> built the city on a peninsula of nine hills at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arak-iran">Araks</a> and Metsamor rivers, in the Ostan Hayotsʻ canton of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayrarat">Ayrarat</a>, perhaps on the remains of an older Urartian settlement. The name is of Iranian origin, a shortening of Artaxšas-šāt, the joy of Artaxias. Its citadel stood on the height later called Khor Virap, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-the-illuminator">Gregory the Illuminator</a> was imprisoned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiridates-iii-of-armenia">Tiridates III of Armenia</a>. Strabo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> call it the Armenian Carthage and tell an apocryphal story that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> chose the site.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations show paved streets, bathhouses, markets, workshops and administrative buildings, with a treasury, a mint and a customs house; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>'s first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> was built here. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Syrians lived in the city, which stood on trade routes linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>. Tigranes II moved his capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranocerta">Tigranocerta</a>, but Artaxata withstood Lucullus in 69 BCE and an assault by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>. To save it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, Tigranes submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. Mark Antony marched on it in 34 BCE and took King Artavasdes II captive.</p><p>After Corbulo razed it, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> sent architects to help Tiridates I rebuild it, and it was briefly renamed Neroneia. Trajan placed a Roman garrison there, and Roman forces under Statius Priscus destroyed it again in 163/64. The Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a> ravaged it in the late 360s. After the partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> it was one of three authorised points of trade between Sasanian Iran and the Eastern Roman Empire. In 449 Armenia's leaders met there in council against the threats of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-ii">Yazdegerd II</a>, and the city was destroyed once more in the rebellion that followed. It then declined as Duin rose, and shifting rivers and floods probably led to its abandonment.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The site was identified in the 1920s, and excavations began in 1970. It lies 8 kilometres south of the modern city of Artashat in the Ararat Province, near the monastery of Khor Virap, and remains of the city walls and some buildings are still visible. In 2024 archaeologists from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> found there the remains of Armenia's oldest known church, an octagonal building dated to the mid-4th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Artaxata” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Strabo and Plutarch say Hannibal was received at the court of Artaxias I and, in a story they also tell, planned and supervised the building of <b>Artaxata</b>, though the tale is reckoned apocryphal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings ruled from <b>Artaxata</b> and later Vagharshapat over powerful hereditary noble houses such as the Mamikonians and Bagratunis.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-armenia-arsacid">Kingdom of Armenia (Arsacid)</a></li></ul></section>
